CRITERION (b) every step holds as written -- FAIL (step 7 / step 4, one defect; everything else refuted, list below)
 F1. Cleanup is not signal-safe: `cleanup()` starts with `trap - EXIT INT TERM` (swarm/codex-check.sh:154), so ONE signal during the ~5-10 s teardown
     window (`compose logs`, `compose down --timeout 5`, docker rm/network rm) kills the script before `scrub_audit` (line 159) and before `rm -rf "$TMP"`.
     Repro A (the normal path, ONE signal): fixture repo + stub that echoes a fake auth token into an event, writes it to out/leak.txt and returns a valid
       PASS; host side waits until a `docker compose ... down` process of that project exists, then `kill -TERM <harness bash pid>` once
       (scratch: h/sig2.sh, run dir sig14).
       Result: harness rc=143; T.14.checker-codex.verdict WAS published; afterwards `grep -rlaF <token>` finds
         sig14/swarm/codex/T.14/out/leak.txt, sig14/swarm/codex/T.14/events.jsonl (both UNREDACTED) and codex-check.FL74c0/auth.snapshot (a full copy of the
         auth file, left in the temp dir together with the copy of the tree). A re-run at the same (task, attempt) exits at step 0 on the existing verdict,
         so nothing ever cleans that audit dir; it is the archive candidate of ruling CD-e.
     Repro B (two signals mid-run, sig8): TERM, then TERM 1 s later -> same leftovers (leak.txt, events.jsonl, auth.snapshot, work copy). A single
       TERM mid-run (sig7 used HUP: rc=129, skip written, no secret anywhere, no temp dir) is handled correctly; only the cleanup window is unprotected.
     Remedy verified (rule 6b): in my copy add `trap '' INT TERM HUP` right after line 154 and repeat Repro A (sig15): harness rc=0, verdict written,
       0 secrets under the audit dir, 0 temp dirs left. So the defect is exactly the reset trap, inside CD1 territory, one line.
     Why this is inside the written contract: step 7 says "Before the script exits, on every path", step 4 "torn down on every exit path"; the worker
       built INT/TERM handling for exactly this reason, and the oracle/tests treat interruption as an exit path. The SAME class as attempt-1's F1a/F1b
       (a path on which the scrub does not run). It needs no unusual privilege: `timeout 1800 codex-check.sh`, Ctrl-C twice, or a session teardown.
 Related findings, same secret-hygiene class (I do NOT rest the FAIL on them; each is letter-ambiguous or exotic):
 F2. classify truncates BEFORE it redacts. tools.py cmd_classify: `Secrets(...).text(one_line(detail, 240))`. Repro (run dir f3): stub emits
     {"type":"error","message":"401 Unauthorized: invalid token <443-char fake JWT>"} and exits 1 -> skip `auth` whose DETAIL carries the first 205 of the
     443 secret characters verbatim (measured). With a 35-char secret starting at column ~215 (f4) the DETAIL ends `tok_FAKE_SECRET_012...` (20 of 35).
     skip() in the shell does redact-then-cut correctly; classify does the reverse. Remedy verified docker-free: `one_line(Secrets(...).text(detail), 240)`
     -> `... invalid token [REDACTED]`. Letter: "occurrence" of a secret is the whole value, so a prefix is arguable; real Codex 401 text does not quote tokens.
 F3. Escaped secret used as a JSON KEY is not detected/redacted (strings_in walks values only): `tools.py secrets clean` left
     {"tok...":1} and {"tok_FAKE_...":true} intact while the same escapes as VALUES were redacted. Exotic.
 F4. Non-listed encodings are not redacted (beyond the brief's raw / JSON-escaped / decoded forms, so not a defect): base64, hex, reversed, rot13,
     percent-encoding, upper/lower-cased, split by newline/space/NUL/CR, prefix-only. UTF-16 LE/BE/BOM, `\/`-escaped, \uXXXX-escaped values, a
     double-encoded JSON string and pretty-printed multi-line JSON WERE redacted.
 F5. Symlinks created by the container under out/ survive when their TARGET is a host path (e.g. out/hostauth-link -> <host auth path>, out/hostdir-link);
     the harness never follows them (host file sha256/inode/mtime/mode unchanged, 0400 file next to the link intact) but a later `cp -rL`/`zip`/`tar -h`
     of the audit dir would read the host file. Not "a secret in the dir"; a hazard for CD4's archive step.

 Everything else in (b) was attacked and REFUTED (command -> result):
 - HOST/LAN reachability. Network `isolated`: internal, enable_ipv6 false, com.docker.network.bridge.inhibit_ipv4=true (docker network inspect). Container: eth0 has
   only 172.25.0.x/16, no default route (/proc/net/route), CapEff 0, seccomp on. From the checker, Python/bash: TCP to the host bridge's IPv6 link-local
   fe80::... (%eth0) on my dual-stack host listener port, :8080 and :3000 -> ENETUNREACH (eth0 has disable_ipv6=1 and no fe80; if_inet6 shows only lo, so the
   host's fe80 address on the bridge, visible with `ip -br addr`, is unreachable). IPv4 192.168.1.90 / 172.17.0.1 / 100.73.80.36 / 172.25.0.1 / 127.0.0.1 with
   plain connect -> unreachable. Harder: SO_BINDTODEVICE=eth0 (unprivileged, makes Linux assume on-link) to the same addresses, TCP and UDP to a host UDP
   listener: ARP for host addresses stays incomplete (/proc/net/arp flags 0x0), TCP -> EHOSTUNREACH, host UDP listener log empty (TCP listener log too); the only
   answering neighbour is the proxy (RST on the probe port). Raw/packet/ICMPv6 sockets -> EPERM, unshare -Urn -> EPERM, no docker.sock. DNS: getent and raw UDP to
   127.0.0.11: chatgpt.com, example.com, a unique name -> SERVFAIL; host.docker.internal / gateway.docker.internal -> NXDOMAIN; only `proxy` resolves.
 - Proxy as a pivot (raw sockets from the checker to proxy:8888, host-side canary listener + proxy log): CONNECT to 172.26.0.1 (egress gateway = host),
   192.168.1.90, 172.17.0.1, 100.73.80.36, 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1], 0.0.0.0, 2130706433, 0x7f000001, host.docker.internal on ports 443/8080/<listener> ->
   403 Filtered / 403 Access violation; absolute-URI GET to the same -> 403; userinfo `chatgpt.com@EGW`, `EGW:port@chatgpt.com`, `#`, backslash, origin-form Host EGW,
   Host/URL mismatches both ways, X-Forwarded-Host, pipelined second request after CONNECT, keep-alive pipelining, tab in host, trailing dot, IPv6 zone id, HTTP/0.9,
   tinyproxy.stats, `proxy:8888` itself -> either 403 or a normal request to chatgpt.com (proxy log: "opening connection to chatgpt.com"); canary listener got ZERO
   hits (its log only says "listening"). Proxy container has ip_forward=1 but the checker has no route or NET_ADMIN to use it as a router; ARP for non-local
   addresses is not answered (proxy_arp=0, accept_source_route=0). Uppercase CHATGPT.COM:443 is accepted (same host).
