OBSERVATION on the worker's note (read-only auth.json cannot stop the real Codex from consuming a refresh token): agreed, and it matters more than the note says.
 - The mount protects the file's bytes, not the credential: a Codex that decides to refresh (its binary carries the strings "Failed to refresh token" / "Your access
   token could not be refreshed"; my recollection, unverified here, is that codex-rs refreshes when last_refresh is older than ~8 days or on a 401) POSTs the refresh
   token to auth.openai.com (an allowlisted host). Refresh tokens are rotated server-side; the container cannot persist the new one on a ro file, so the HOST
   file keeps a now-dead refresh token and the user's own Codex login goes stale (next host use: sign in again). The old rw design would have persisted it (and was
   rejected for the truncation hazard); a copy would be equally stale. Only a pre-run freshness gate helps: skip `auth` with an actionable DETAIL when the access
   token's exp / last_refresh is near, and tell the user to run Codex once on the host first. Suggest documenting it in CD4. Not grounds for FAIL (the brief
   mandates ro and says a stale token yields skip `auth`).
OTHER OBSERVATIONS (not grounds for FAIL)
 - The harness never checks at run time that the host really has no IPv4 address on the isolated bridge: on a Docker daemon that ignores the inhibit_ipv4 option the
   old gateway exposure returns silently. (Here: Docker 29.2.1; the bridge carries only fe80::/64, which the container cannot use.) Consider a runtime `docker network inspect`/probe.
 - Container keeps Docker's default bounding caps (CapBnd a80425fb incl. NET_RAW), setuid-root binaries (su, mount, passwd, ...) exist in the image, no cap_drop/no-new-privileges,
   no pids/memory/disk limits. CapEff is 0 and I found no escalation, but the isolation of the host rests on "no root in the container"; cap_drop ALL + no-new-privileges is cheap.
 - `out/` is Codex-writable content inside the host tree (symlinks, exec bits, a nested .git with core.fsmonitor) - see F5.
 - /proc/self/mountinfo inside the container shows host source paths (user name, worktree path). Container resolv.conf carries the host's tailnet search domain.
 - A SIGHUP-terminated run writes the skip DETAIL "harness failed (exit 0) before producing a result" (cosmetic, rc=129 is right).
